An HTML structure is the foundational skeleton of a webpage that tells web browsers how to organize, interpret, and display content. Every standard webpage relies on a fundamental hierarchy of nested tags to establish this layout.

The Standard HTML Boilerplate

Below is the universal structural template required for any compliant HTML5 document:

html

<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Page Title</title>
</html >
<body>
    <header>
        <h1>Welcome to My Website</h1>
    </header>
    <main>
        <p>This is where the main content goes.</p>
    </main>
    <footer>
        <p>© 2026 My Company</p>
    </footer>
</body>
</html>


Use code with caution.
Essential Root Elements
<!DOCTYPE html>: The document type declaration. It forces modern browsers to render pages using the latest HTML5 web standards.
<html>: The root element of the page. It wraps all other structural components, and its lang attribute specifies the primary text language.


The Invisible Section: Head

The <head> tag contains background information (metadata) about the site that does not physically appear on the screen.
<meta charset="UTF-8">: Configures character encoding to ensure text and special symbols render without glitches.
<meta name="viewport">: Configures screen dimensions to make layouts fully responsive across mobile devices.
<title>: Sets the specific title displayed directly on the browser's upper tab.
Resource Links: Houses references to external assets like CSS stylesheets or JavaScript logic.

The Visible Section: Body

The <body> tag acts as the master container for all visible content served to your site visitors.
<header>: Groups navigational links, logos, and top-level branding.
<main>: Encloses the primary, unique substance of the specific document.
<footer>: Contains copyright info, secondary links, or contact data.


Core Content Block Elements


<h1> to <h6>: Renders section headings, moving hierarchically from most to least important.
<p>: Packages blocks of regular running text into distinct paragraphs.
<a>: Establishes functional hyperlinks using an anchoring target address.
<img>: Embeds visual image media assets into the layout structure.


