HTML elements are the foundational building blocks of a webpage that define the structure, semantics, and layout of its content. An element typically consists of a start tag, the inner content, and an end tag


<p class="text">This is the content inside a paragraph element.</p>
│       │       └───────────────────┬───────────────────┘ └──┬──┘
│       └─ Attribute                │                        └─ Closing Tag
└─ Opening Tag                      └─ Element Content


Component Breakdown

Opening Tag: The element name enclosed in angle brackets (e.g., <p>), which signals where the element begins.
Attributes: Key-value pairs placed inside the opening tag to provide additional settings or metadata (e.g., class="text", href="").
Content: The text, images, or nested elements nested between the tags.
Closing Tag: The element name preceded by a forward slash (e.g., </p>), signaling the end of the element.

