Html Attributes
HTML attributes provide additional information, configuration, and behavioral controls for HTML elements. They are always placed inside the opening tag of an element and typically appear as name="value" pairs.

Structure of an Attribute

<tagname attribute_name="attribute_value">Content</tagname>

Attribute Name: Identifies what property you are setting (e.g., href, src, class).
Attribute Value: Specifies the actual configuration, typically wrapped in double quotes.



Core Categories of Attributes

1. Global Attributes
These can be used on any HTML element.
id: Assigns a unique identifier to target an element with CSS or JavaScript.
class: Assigns non-unique classes to group elements for collective CSS styling.
style: Applies inline CSS rules directly to the element.
title: Adds extra context that pops up as a tooltip on hover.
lang: Declares the primary language of the element's content.


2. Element-Specific Attributes
These work only with specific tags to fulfill their functional requirements.
Links (<a>): Use href to declare the destination URL.
Images (<img>): Use src for the image path, alt for backup descriptive text, and width/height for layout dimensions.
Forms (<form>): Use action to establish the data destination and method to set the HTTP submission type.
Inputs (<input>): Use type to toggle input fields (e.g., text, password, checkbox, file).



3. Boolean Attributes
These attributes alter element state simply by being present in the opening tag. No value assignment is necessary.
disabled: Deactivates user interaction for form controls.
checked: Marks checkboxes or radio buttons as active by default.
required: Mandates that a form field must be filled before submission.



4. Custom Data Attributes
data-*: Allows developers to store custom, private metadata directly on standard HTML elements to easily exchange data with JavaScript.


