HTML forms are used to collect user input on web pages and send that data to a server for processing. Every form is defined using the wrapper <form> tag, which encloses various interactive controls like text boxes, checkboxes, radio buttons, and submit buttons. Over 85% of websites rely on forms to handle crucial user interactions like logging in, registration, or sending search queries.

Core Form Attributes

The <form> element requires two main structural attributes to manage data submission properly:

#Action: Specifies the URL or server endpoint where the data is sent upon submission.
#method: Defines the HTTP method used to transfer data, typically GET or POST.
  
Attribute Value            Visibility                                                         Security                                                                                     Data Limits                                                      Common Use Case
GET                       Appends data directly to the  URL                  None (Visible in address bar)                                                  Limited by URL length                                                  Search fields                                                                    
POST                   Embeds data inside the HTTP request body        Better protection for data transits                                             No strict limits                                                               Password fields and logins
            
            
Key Form Elements & Elements Controls

A basic form consists of distinct semantic elements working together to collect information:

<label>: Provides an explicit text descriptor for a control, crucial for web accessibility.
<input>: The most common and flexible form element, customized entirely by its type attribute.
<textarea>: Creates a multi-line text input field used for comments, reviews, or longer descriptions.
<select> & <option>: Creates a dropdown menu selection layer.
<button>: Triggers actions like form clearing or data submission.

Common Input Types

The <input> control alters its entire functionality depending on the type declaration:

type="text": Default single-line textbox for general words or names.
type="password": Masks typed text to protect highly sensitive information.
type="email": Automatically validates that the user enters a syntactically correct email structure.
type="checkbox": Allows users to check off multiple items simultaneously from a single group.
type="radio": Forces users to select only one choice out of a pre-configured group.
type="submit": Renders a button that packages data and transmits it to the server endpoint.   

