DOM:
A DOM tree (Document Object Model tree) is a hierarchical, branching structure created by web browsers to represent an HTML or XML document in memory. It converts raw source code tags into functional, programmable objects that languages like JavaScript can read, modify, and style in real time.

Structure and Anatomy


The DOM tree organizes a web page into parent-child relationships, mirroring how your code is nested:

Document (Root Node): The entry point into the tree representing the entire page.
Element Nodes: The structural HTML tags (e.g., <html>, <body>, <div>).
Text Nodes: The literal text contained within an element.
Attribute Nodes: Key-value data inside tags, like href or class.
Comment Nodes: Hidden annotations left in your HTML code.

Visual Representation

If you have a basic HTML document, its DOM layout transforms like this:

<!-- HTML Code -->
<html>
  <head>
    <title>My Page</title>
  </head>
  <body>
    <h1>Hello World</h1>
  </head>
</html>


<!-- Corresponding DOM Tree Structure -->
                 [Document]
                       │
                   <html>
        ┌─────┴─────┐
     <head>                   <body>
        │                             │
     <title>                       <h1>
        │                              │
    "My Page"            "Hello World"


Tree Node Relationships

Nodes are described using family tree terminology based on their structural layout:
Parent: A node directly above another (e.g., <body> is the parent of <h1>).
Child: A node directly inside another (e.g., <title> is a child of <head>).
Sibling: Nodes sharing the exact same parent (e.g., <head> and <body> are siblings).
Descendant: Any node nested deep inside another at any lower tier.
                         
